        "utm_medium=referral&utm_source=stackoverflow-community&utm_campaign=side-bar&utm_content=explore-teams-compact-popover)\n\n# URL for latest stable Go release\n\nAsk Question\n\nAsked 7 years, 11 months ago\n\nModified [30 days ago](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/51933636/url-for-latest-stable-go-release?lastactivity \"2026-06-20 02:43:43Z\")\n\nViewed 14k times\n\n26\n\nIs there is a permanent URL that points to current latest linux binary release of Go?\n\nI am writing an ansible script which should download the latest Go release and install it. In the Go download site \"https://golang.org/dl/\" I could see only release specific download links.\n\nI am wondering if there a link like \"https://dl.google.com/go/latest.linux-amd64.tar.gz\" available?\n\nIf not any suggestion on how to script fetching the latest golang version and install it?\n\n* go\n\nShare\n\nImprove this question\n\nFollow\n\nedited Oct 12, 2022 at 10:22\n\nJonathan Hall's user avatar\n\nJonathan Hall\n\n80\\.\n7k 19 19 gold badges 163 163 silver badges 207 207 bronze badges\n\nasked Aug 20, 2018 at 15:06\n\nMurli's user avatar\n\nMurli\n\n885 1 1 gold badge 7 7 silver badges 16 16 bronze badges\n\nAdd a comment | \n\n## 6 Answers 6\n\nSorted by: [Reset to default](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/51933636/url-for-latest-stable-go-release?answertab=scoredesc)\n\nHighest score (default) Trending (recent votes count more) Date modified (newest first) Date created (oldest first)\n\n31\n\nYou can download the latest stable Go version in one go :)\n\n**UPDATED** (on 2026-06-20):\n\n```\ncurl -OL \"https://dl.google.com/go/$(curl -s https://go.dev/VERSION\\?m\\=text | head -1).linux-$( uname -m | sed 's/x86_64/amd64/;s/aarch64/arm64/').tar.gz\"\n```\n\nShare\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow\n\nedited Jun 20 at 2:43\n\nanswered Aug 30, 2019 at 10:50\n\nnad2000's user avatar\n\nnad2000\n\n5,044 1 1 gold badge 34 34 silver badges 25 25 bronze badges\n\nSign up to request clarification or add additional context in comments.\n\n## 6 Comments\nAdd a comment\n\nCaster\n\nCaster Over a year ago\n\n`wget \"https://dl.google.com/go/$(curl 'https://golang.org/VERSION?m=text').linux-amd64.tar.gz\"` useful\n\n2020-12-02T06:39:30.99Z+00:00\n\n0 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\nShabirmean\n\nShabirmean Over a year ago\n\nThis method doesn't work anymore. The URLs have changed. `https://go.dev/dl/<VERSION>` and for fetching version `https://go.dev/VERSION?m=text`\n\n2022-04-12T19:40:51.213Z+00:00\n\n4 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\nBarumpus\n\nBarumpus Over a year ago\n\n@Shabirmean the /VERSION?m=text url is not the right one to use -- it returns the version of go backing the go.dev website, which right now is 1.18.1, a bleed edge version that is not yet available for download! The URL you really want to use is `https://go.dev/dl/?mode=json` . See [github.com/golang/go/issues/51135](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/51135) for details.\n\n2022-04-13T00:20:40.26Z+00:00\n\n3 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\nthomasmaclean\n\nthomasmaclean Over a year ago\n\n`curl \"https://dl.google.\ncom/go/$(curl https://go.dev/dl/?mode=json | grep -o 'go.*.linux-amd64.tar.gz' | head -n 1 | tr -d '\\r\\n' )\"`\n\n2023-03-17T19:55:10.023Z+00:00\n\n1 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\nstefanlindbohm\n\nstefanlindbohm Over a year ago\n\nA simpler version taking care of the additional lines in the response: `wget \"https://dl.google.com/go/$(curl https://go.dev/VERSION?m=text | head -n1).linux-amd64.tar.gz\"`\n\n2024-03-06T18:36:36.497Z+00:00\n\n3 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\nAdd a comment\n\n6\n\nAs found [here](https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/tools/cmd/getgo) , Google has a Linux installer to install Go on linux:\n\n[https://storage.googleapis.com/golang/getgo/installer\\_linux](https://storage.googleapis.com/golang/getgo/installer_linux)\n\nThis installer fetches the latest version of Go and installs it. Seems like this is the easiest way as of now to install the latest go version on Linux.\n\nThe newest official method to fetch the file and execute it is:\n\n```\ncurl -LO https://get.golang.org/$(uname)/go_installer\nchmod +x go_installer\n./go_installer\nrm go_installer\n```\n\nShare\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow\n\nedited Sep 23, 2022 at 15:58\n\nAlexis Wilke's user avatar\n\nAlexis Wilke\n\n21\\.4k 11 11 gold badges 112 112 silver badges 186 186 bronze badges\n\nanswered Aug 22, 2018 at 13:17\n\nMurli's user avatar\n\nMurli\n\n885 1 1 gold badge 7 7 silver badges 16 16 bronze badges\n\n## 1 Comment\n\nAdd a comment\n\nBenjamin\n\nBenjamin Over a year ago\n\nIt seems the package is broken and not actively maintained. Someone decided to add a second line encompassing the date to the API response which this installer does not expect. But as others have noted here in this Q&A, the retrieved version number is ithe version the website is running, it does not have to be the latest stable you are looking for. Here is a workaround: `curl -LO get.golang.org/$(uname)/go_installer && chmod +x go_installer && ./go_installer --version $(curl https://go.dev/dl/?mode=json | jq -r '.[0].version') && rm go_installer`\n\n2023-09-11T23:43:06.\n347Z+00:00\n\n1 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\n4\n\nYou can generate the latest url with :\n\n```\nhttps://dl.google.com/go{{ version }}.{{ os }}-{{ arch }}.tar.gz\n```\n\n**os:** linux, darwin, windows, freebsd  \n**arch:** amd64, 386, armv6l, arm64, s390, ppc64le\n\nand for the latest stable version you can fetch the value with curl or something else from the url :\n\n```\nhttps://golang.org/VERSION?m=text\n```\n\nHere is an ansible playbook as an example :\n\n```\n---\n- hosts: server\n  gather_facts: no\n\n  vars:\n    version : \"latest\"\n    arch: arm64\n    os: linux\n\n    latest_version_url: https://golang.org/VERSION?m=text\n    archive_name: \"{{ filename_prefix }}.{{ os }}-{{ arch }}.tar.gz\"\n    download_url: https://dl.google.com/go/{{ archive_name }}\n    bin_path: /usr/local/go/bin\n\n  tasks:\n    - name: Get filename prefix with latest version\n      set_fact:\n        filename_prefix: \"{{ lookup('url', latest_version_url, split_lines=False) }}\"\n      when: version == \"latest\"\n- name: Get filename prefix with fixed version\n      set_fact:\n        filename_prefix: go{{ version }}\n      when: version != \"latest\"\n    \n    - name: Try to get current go version installed\n      command: go version\n      register: result\n      changed_when: false\n\n    - name: Set current_version var to the current\n      set_fact:\n        current_version: \"{{ result.stdout.split(' ')[2] }}\"\n      when: result.failed == false\n\n    - name: Set current_version var to none\n      set_fact:\n        current_version: \"none\"\n      when: result.failed == true\n\n    - debug:\n        var: current_version\n\n    - name: Download and extract the archive {{ archive_name }}\n      become: true\n      unarchive:\n          src: \"{{ download_url }}\"\n          dest: /usr/local\n          remote_src: yes\n      when: current_version != filename_prefix\n```\n\nShare\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow\n\nedited Sep 23, 2022 at 4:18\n\nAlexis Wilke's user avatar\n\nAlexis Wilke\n\n21\\.\n4k 11 11 gold badges 112 112 silver badges 186 186 bronze badges\n\nanswered May 16, 2019 at 22:52\n\nfhusson's user avatar\n\nfhusson\n\n334 4 4 silver badges 8 8 bronze badges\n\n## 2 Comments\n\nAdd a comment\n\nMachavity\n\nMachavity Over a year ago\n\nI think you've misunderstood the question. They don't want the latest version _number_ , they want the latest version _installer_\n\n2019-05-17T18:07:17.823Z+00:00\n\n0 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\nfhusson\n\nfhusson Over a year ago\n\nYou are right, just the url <https://golang.org/VERSION?m=text> is not enough, but it was the missing part from the other answer to make a script, I have updated my answer to be more complete.\n\n2019-05-18T19:31:40.163Z+00:00\n\n0 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\n4\n\nEdit 2025-11: If you are looking here I strongly recommend you take a look at <https://mise.jdx.dev/getting-started.html>\n\n```\n# Install golang with a particular version number.\nmise use -g go@latest\n# Install a tool from the mise registry.\nmise use -g k9s\n# Install a tool that is not in the mise registry, but offers pre-built binaries through services like GitHub releases.\nmise use -g ubi:derailed/k9s\n```\n\nI'm not affiliated with the project. It only made me stop writing poor shell code myself.\n\n* * *\n\nIt seems like many answers here are outdated and make wrong assumptions.\n\n<https://github.com/golang/go/issues/51135>\n\n> VERSION is the version running the website, not latest version to download.\n> \n> \n\n# Download for Linux\n\n```\nos=\"linux\"\narch=\"amd64\"\next=\"tar.gz\"\ncurl -LO go.dev/dl/$(curl https://go.dev/dl/?mode=json \\\n                     | jq -r '.[0].version').$os-$arch.$ext\n```\n\n(On Windows you have your winget and on MacOS you have your Homebrew. I don't know why you are here reading this.)\n\n# Installer for Linux\n\nAnd here is the workaround for the installer Murli posted.\n\n```\ncurl -LO get.golang.org/$(uname)/go_installer \\\n&& chmod +x go_installer \\\n&& ./go_installer \\\n     --version $(curl https://go.dev/dl/?mode=json \\\n\n...\n\n```\n\nThere is also a [cross-platform solution built by the Go Community](https://go.googlesource.com/tools/+/master/cmd/getgo/) :\n\n```\ncurl -LO https://get.golang.org/$(uname)/go_installer && chmod +x go_installer && ./go_installer && rm go_installer\n```\n\nShare\n\nImprove this answer\n\nFollow\n\nanswered Jul 14, 2022 at 7:59\n\n17xande's user avatar\n\n17xande\n\n3,013 3 3 gold badges 28 28 silver badges 33 33 bronze badges\n\n## 2 Comments\n\nAdd a comment\n\nWillem\n\nWillem Over a year ago\n\nDoes not work for arm64\n\n2022-11-24T09:21:10.133Z+00:00\n\n0 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\n17xande\n\n17xande Over a year ago\n\nNo it won't, I hard-coded it for amd64 because that's what I use. Just replace `amd64` with `arm64` in the script above and it should work.\n\n2022-11-29T10:01:03.117Z+00:00\n\n0 Reply\n\n* Copy link\n\n0\n\nIn case someone comes here looking for a direct, valid download URL for the \\*\\*latest stable Go release\\*\\*, you can always find it by checking the filenames available at:\n\n<https://go.dev/dl/>"
